※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ The startup function may take what the program was started with, and what that
※ is is the record the `std` module calls `Init`.
※
※ **What is handed over is where it is.**  The record itself is an object the
※ image carries, in the writable data, so a program may change what it was
※ started with -- and so the record may grow, as the arguments and the
※ environment will make it grow, without this signature changing.
※
※ **A descriptor and not a number.**  `init.io.output` is a `Writer`, which is a
※ record around the number the kernel knows; what says a thing may be written is
※ its type, and there is no way to hand a `Reader` to `write`.  Three descriptors
※ arrive open, in the order every system opens them.
※
※ **One mutable reference and no lock.**  `&mut init.io.output` is exclusive by
※ the rule that already refuses a second `&mut` to a place, so nothing is checked
※ while the program runs and nothing is locked.  What it costs is that lending
※ one field lends the whole of `init` for as long as the reference lives, the
※ aliasing rule not telling one field of a local from another -- which is written
※ down as a thing to fix and not as a thing to live with.
※
※ **`Init` rather than the descriptors outright**, so that the arguments, the
※ environment and whatever else a program inherits have somewhere to go without
※ changing the one signature every program writes.
※
※ Compare: **C**, whose `main` takes the arguments and whose three descriptors
※ are integers anyone may write to at any time; **Rust**, whose `std::io::stdout`
※ is taken by a call and guarded by a lock, a run-time check where a static one
※ is available; **Go**, whose `os.Stdout` is a package variable; **Zig**, which
※ passes a writer explicitly as this does.

let std := ⎕import("std")

@[startup, impure]
fn main(init: mut std.Init) → u6:
    let message: u8⟦6⟧ = ⟦104u8, 101u8, 108u8, 108u8, 111u8, 10u8⟧ in ⎕static
    ※ the numbers the kernel knows them by, in the order they arrive
    if init.io.input.fd ≠ 0i32:
        1u6
    elif init.io.output.fd ≠ 1i32:
        2u6
    elif init.io.error.fd ≠ 2i32:
        3u6
    else:
        ※ and one of them written through, which answers how many bytes went or
        ※ why none did
        match std.write_sync(&mut init.io.error, message):
            u64 ¤size(n):
                if n ≠ 6:
                    4u6
                else:
                    ※ what the program was started with may be changed, it
                    ※ being an object the program holds and not a value it was
                    ※ handed: `output` is made to be where `errors` goes
                    init.io.output ← std.Writer(.fd ← 2i32)
                    if init.io.output.fd = 2i32: 0u6 else: 6u6
            ⊥:
                5u6
